# Agent and Model Router

Classify every task before execution by complexity, risk, scope, and uncertainty; use the smallest capable agent/model. Full procedure and team setup: `route-task` skill.

| Tier | Model | Typical work | Scope |
|---|---|---|---|
| S | fast/low-cost | renames, lint fixes, obvious localized bugs, simple tests/CRUD on an existing pattern, docs/config, search | 1–3 files; no schema, security, or ambiguous business decision |
| M | balanced | normal features, multi-file fixes, new endpoint on existing architecture, moderate refactors, low/medium-risk migrations | 3–12 files, one bounded module |
| L | strongest | new/cross-module architecture, complex DB design, unclear business rules, high-impact performance, production incidents, broad legacy refactors | >12 files, several contexts, or high uncertainty |
| XL | lead + team | multiple independent workstreams, risky production migration/critical release — stand up via `run-project-team` | multi-workstream |

**Risk overrides — always ≥ Tier L regardless of size:** payments/refunds/balances/financial records; authentication/authorization/roles/IDOR/tenant isolation; secrets, personal data, uploads, webhooks, SSRF; destructive or irreversible data changes; concurrency/idempotency/locks/races; production incidents; public API compatibility.

Escalate immediately on new uncertainty, risk, or scope. Small agents stop and hand off at architecture, sensitive flows, or unclear business rules. Strong agents delegate mechanical subtasks to smaller agents. Never split a tiny task across multiple agents.
